      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Edwin Land's Early Life</strong><br>\nEdwin Land was born in 1909 in Connecticut, USA. From a young age, he was fascinated with light, which led him to experiment with polarized filters in high school. He attended Harvard University but dropped out after his freshman year to focus on his research. Land's early experiments with light polarization resulted in his first patent and laid the groundwork for future innovations in photography and optics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Invention of Polarized Film</strong><br>\nIn 1929, Edwin Land developed the first synthetic polarizer, which could polarize light without using large crystals. This invention was significant because it allowed for the production of polarized film in large sheets. Land's polarizing film later became widely used in sunglasses, photography, and scientific equipment, revolutionizing how light and images are managed and viewed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding of Polaroid Corporation</strong><br>\nIn 1937, Edwin Land founded the Polaroid Corporation. The company initially produced polarizing filters for scientific and military use. During World War II, Polaroid worked on several defense projects, including goggles and targeting devices for soldiers. The success of these products established Polaroid as a leader in optical technology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Breakthrough with Instant Photography</strong><br>\nLand's interest in photography led to a major breakthrough in 1947 when he introduced the concept of instant photography. He developed a camera that could produce a photograph within a minute after taking the picture. This invention changed the landscape of photography, making it possible for people to immediately see the images they captured.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Polaroid Camera Goes Public</strong><br>\nThe first Polaroid camera was sold to the public in 1948. It was a hit, with its ability to produce photos almost instantly being its main attraction. Land continued to improve the technology, and new models of Polaroid cameras were released over the years, becoming iconic products in the photography world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Patents and Innovations</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Edwin Land was a prolific inventor. He held over 500 patents, covering various aspects of photography, light polarization, and optics. His work not only included the development of the instant camera but also contributed to the invention of the color polarization process among other optical technologies.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
